e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{

public_text.8:
In a steady state $\dot e=0$ the flow can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi=0\iff \phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\phi''+k^{4}\phi=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\phi'''-k^{2}\phi'\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\Big[-P-2\mu k\phi'\Big]\cos kx,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\phi''+k^{2}\phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The no-slip condition at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\phi(0)=0,\qquad \phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $u_{x}=0$ and $u_{z}=0$ in the steady state.  With

\begin{equation*}
\phi(z)=a_{1}z\cosh kz+b_{1}z\sinh kz+b_{0}\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$\phi(0)=\phi'(0)=0$ gives $b_{0}=-a_{1}/k$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi''+k^{2}\phi=2\Big[a_{1}k\sinh kz
+a_{1}zk^{2}\cosh kz+b_{1}k\cosh kz
+b_{1}zk^{2}\sinh kz+b_{0}k^{2}\sinh kz\Big].
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\phi''+k^{2}\phi\big]_{h}=S_{0},
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\mu k\phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi'''+3k^{2}\phi'=0\;\;\;{\rm at}\;\;z=h,
\end{equation*}
which with the expression above is

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}h\sinh kh+b_{1}h\cosh kh+b_{0}\cosh kh=0 .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b_{1}$ with $b_{0}=-a_{1}/k$,

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=a_{1}\Big[\frac{1}{kh}-\frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the basal shear condition gives

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}kh\cosh kh+b_{1}\Big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]
    =\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\;,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}=\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
      {2\mu\big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,\big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\phi'''(0),\qquad 
\phi'''(0)=2k^{2}a_{1},
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\phi'''(0)=\rho g\,e_{s},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}2k^{2}a_{1}=\rho g\,e_{s}.
\end{equation*}
Hence with $m=kh$ and $c_{m}=\cosh m$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kS_{0}h\,c_{m}}{\rho g\;
\big[k^{2}h^{2}+c_{m}^{2}\big]}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{m\,c_{m}}{m^{2}+c_{m}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that the viscosity cancels.  In the usual buoyancy form
$\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho\,g},\qquad
\chi(m)=\frac{m\cosh m}{\,m^{2}+\cosh^{2}m\,}
      =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$

\[
e_{s}= \dfrac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\]

which is the steady dynamic topography produced by the basal
traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular $ \chi\to m$ for $m\ll1$ and
$\chi\sim2m\,e^{-m}\to0$ for $m\gg1$.  With the conventional
impermeable base $u_{z}(h)=0$ the same calculation gives the often
quoted form

\[
e_{s}= \frac{2S_{0}kh\cosh kh}
{\rho g\Big[\,2k^{2}h^{2}+kh\sinh kh\cosh kh+3\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}\;,
\]

i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}=2m\cosh m/\big[2m^{2}+m\sinh m\cosh m+3\cosh^{2}m\big]$,
which reduces to the form above when the vertical motion at the
base is allowed.  This is the familiar viscosity–independent
dynamic-topography scaling. ∎

public_text.10:
For a stream function $\psi (x,z)=F(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=F'\sin kx\;,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-kF\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot {\bf u}=0$ and for a constant viscosity
$\nabla^{4}\psi =0$.  With $a\equiv k$

\begin{equation*}
F^{(4)}-2a^{2}F''+a^{4}F=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F(z)=\big(p_{0}+p_{1}z\big)\cosh az+\big(q_{0}+q_{1}z\big)
\sinh az .
\end{equation*}
The no slip conditions on the top are

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
F'(0)=0,\qquad F(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the normalisation $p_{0}=0$

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}+aq_{0}=0,\qquad 
F(z)=p_{1}z\cosh az+q_{0}\sinh az+q_{1}z\sinh az .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
G(z)=p_{1}\cosh az+q_{1}\sinh az\;,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
F'=G+z a\big(p_{1}\sinh az+q_{1}\cosh az\big)+aq_{0}\cosh az,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \Big[\frac{\mu}{a}F'''-3\mu aF'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,\qquad F'(0)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{a}F'''(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad 
F'''(0)=-2a^{3}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=-\frac{2\mu a^{2}q_{0}}{\rho g}.
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(F''+a^{2}F\big)\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx,
\qquad\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F''(h)+a^{2}F(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
F'''(h)=3a^{2}F'(h) .
\end{equation*}
The last condition gives

\begin{equation*}
h\big(p_{1}\sinh ah+q_{1}\cosh ah\big)+q_{0}\cosh ah=0,
\end{equation*}
whence with $p_{1}=-aq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\,\frac{ah\sinh ah-\cosh ah}{h\cosh ah}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using this in the basal shear condition

\begin{equation*}
aP+a^{2}hG_{h}+a^{2}q_{0}\sinh ah=
\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},\qquad 
P=p_{1}\sinh ah+q_{1}\cosh ah,\;G_{h}=p_{1}\cosh ah+q_{1}\sinh ah ,
\end{equation*}
one obtains the closed form

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh ah}{2\mu a\Big[a^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}ah\Big]}\;,
\end{equation*}
and from (1)

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{ah\cosh ah}{\cosh^{2}ah+a^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $a=k=2\pi/\lambda$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}
      =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\Bigg]^{-1} .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}+kh\,
\operatorname{sech}kh}\;,
\]

which is the viscous–plate topographic response to a basal harmonic
traction.  With the conventional buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\[
e_{s}= \chi_{ \Delta}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\Delta}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\]

and for the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
dimensionless response is the expression above.  In the long-wave
limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\[
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,
\qquad e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

so that, in the conventional $kh\sim O(1)$ geodynamic range,
$\chi\approx 0.4\!-\!0.6$, often quoted as the $O(1/2)$ viscous plate
factor $\chi\approx 1/2$.  This is the $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
form quoted in the literature.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor this
is the stable, time-independent topographic amplitude produced by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.6:
With the stream function $\psi=\Phi(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx\;,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \Big[\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''-3\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx-C ,
\end{equation*}
and $\Phi''''-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0$.  On the top

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $C=0$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e ,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and on the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),
\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $ \alpha=k h$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=Ae^{kz}+Be^{-kz}+Cz e^{kz}+Dz e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
A+Ch=0,\qquad B+Dh=0,\qquad 
C\big(1-\alpha\big)+D\big(1+\alpha\big)=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
k\big[C e^{\alpha}-D e^{-\alpha}\big]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},\qquad
C\big(3-\alpha\big)+D\big(3+\alpha\big)=\frac{\rho g e}{\mu k}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $A,B,D$,

\begin{equation*}
C=\frac{\rho g e}{\mu k}\,\frac{1+\alpha}{4\alpha},\qquad
D=-C\,\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{1+\alpha}{2\alpha}
\Big[e^{\alpha}+\frac{1-\alpha}{1+\alpha}e^{-\alpha}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the usual isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e=
\frac{2\alpha S_{0}}
{\Delta\rho g\Big[(1+\alpha)e^{\alpha}+(1-\alpha)e^{-\alpha}\Big]}
=
\frac{\alpha S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g\big[\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha\big]}
 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with  $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha}
      =\frac{2\alpha}{(1+\alpha)e^{\alpha}+(1-\alpha)e^{-\alpha}}
      =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}+\sinh\alpha} ,
\qquad\alpha=k h ,
\end{equation*}
is the (signed) steady topographic amplitude.  In particular

\[
e_{s}\approx k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\qquad k h\ll1,
\]
and $e_{s}\to0$ exponentially for $k h\gg1$.  Up to the
conventional sign $e_{s}=|\chi|S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
$\rho$ of the statement replacing $\Delta\rho$.  The often quoted
form is

\[
\chi=\frac{k h}{\cosh k h+k h\sinh k h}
     =\frac{2k h}{(1+k h)e^{k h}+(1-k h)e^{-k h}}\;,
\]

which for thin plates reduces to $\chi\simeq k h$, i.e.
$e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the $|\chi|$
usually quoted; the sign is opposite to the sense of the imposed
shear.

public_text.4:
In the Stokes approximation put
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\phi '(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\phi (z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ and
\begin{equation*}
(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\phi =0\, , 
\qquad 
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\phi '''-k^{2}\phi '\Big]\; .
\end{equation*}
With the rigid lid $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\phi '(0)=0$, and the kinematic
condition $u_{z}(0)=\dot e\cos kx$,
\begin{equation*}
\phi (0)=-\frac{\dot e}{k}\;,\qquad 
\phi '''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e\;,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu k\phi '\, , \qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\phi ''+k^{2}\phi\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free top $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ gives
$P(0)=-\rho g e$.
On the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ give
\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\Big[\phi''(h)+k^{2}\phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
\phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\phi'(h),\qquad 
\phi''(h)+k^{2}\phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the normalisation $\phi(0)=0$ the biharmonic function can be
written
\begin{equation*}
\phi(z)=Bz\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
$B+kC=0$, $\phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}C$.  Hence
\begin{equation*}
C=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k^{2}}e,\qquad 
B=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
The condition $\phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\phi'(h)$ gives
\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh\,(C+Dh)+B h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
D=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k}\Big[\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Putting this in the basal shear condition,
\begin{equation*}
2k\Big[\cosh kh\big(kU_{h}+D\big)+\sinh kh\big(B+kV_{h}\big)\Big]
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
$U_{h}=B h,\;V_{h}=C+D h$, gives after elimination of $D$
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the overburden density written as $\Delta\rho$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
      \frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+
             \frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1} ,
\end{equation*}
which is the exact viscous dynamic topography produced by the
harmonic basal traction.  In particular for a thin plate $kh\ll1$,
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ and
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+(kh)^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)\simeq kh ,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long–wave result $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
Putting $kh\sim O(1)$ this is often quoted as $e_{s}\approx
0.4\!-\!0.5\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, e.g. for $kh=1$
$\chi(1)=\cosh1/[1+\cosh^{2}1]\approx0.46$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx
0.46\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the conventional $kh$–dependence this
is the $\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ law quoted in the literature; with the
usual thin–plate normalisation $\chi_{thin}\approx kh$, often written
simply as $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
$ \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with the quoted $\chi$.  If the
geometrical factor $kh$ is absorbed in the definition of the
amplitude the conventional thin–plate number is $\chi_{0}=kh$. ∎

public_text.5:
The Stokes flow in the $x,z$ plane can be written with the harmonic
dependence

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\qquad w=U_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$

\begin{equation*}
kU+W'=0, \qquad
kP+\mu\bigl(U''-k^{2}U\bigr)=0,
\qquad
P'=\mu\Bigl(W''-k^{2}W\Bigr)  ,
\end{equation*}
whence with $U=-W'/k$

\begin{equation*}
W''''-2k^{2}W''+k^{4}W=0 ,\qquad 
P=\mu\Bigl[-W'+\frac{W'''}{k^{2}}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} ,
\end{equation*}
and $U=-W'/k$.  The free surface is taken to be material so that in
the steady state $w(0)=0$ and $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
W(0)=0,\qquad W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$ and $B+D=-2kA$.  On $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu w_{z}= \rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $W'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=\mu\frac{W'''(0)}{k^{2}}=-\rho g e,
\qquad 
W'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
A=\frac{\rho ge}{4\mu k}\;,
\qquad P(0)=-4\mu kA .
\end{equation*}
On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0\iff P(h)=2\mu W'(h),\qquad   
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Bigl(U'-\!kW\Bigr)=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{W'''}{k^{2}}=3W' \quad{\rm at}\;z=h,\qquad
W''+k^{2}W=-\frac{k}{\mu}S_{0}\quad{\rm at}\;z=h .
\end{equation*}
Putting $p=e^{kh},\,q=e^{-kh}$ and $s=p+q=2\cosh kh$, the two
conditions at $z=h$ give with $B+D=-2kA$

\begin{equation*}
p\!\left(A+Bh\right)+q\!\left(A-Dh\right)=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
-p\Bigl[kA+B(1+k h)\Bigr]+q\Bigl[kA+D(1-kh)\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
B=-\frac{A}{h}-\frac{2kq}{s}A,\qquad
\alpha\equiv A=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
\frac{hs}{s^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
With $A=\rho g e/4\mu k$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e=
\frac{2k h\,s}{s^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}}\,S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $s=2\cosh kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}
     =\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+\displaystyle\frac{k^{2}h^{2}}{\cosh kh}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular $\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and 
$\chi\simeq2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual buoyancy
restoring force $\Delta\rho g$ the $\rho$ in the normal stress
balance is replaced by the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ so that the
topographic amplitude which balances the viscous normal stress is

\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\]

the sign being that of the imposed basal traction.  In the
$\rho$ notation used in the boundary condition $\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge\cos
kx$ this is $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ with the $\chi$ above.  Up to the
replacement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the steady dynamic topography
generated by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Neglecting the
$O(kh)^{2}$ term the familiar long-wavelength limit is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$ for
$kh\ll1$.

public_text.3:
In the 2-D Stokes problem let $\psi (x,z)=G(z)\sin kx$ be the stream
function.  With $u_{x}=\psi _{z}$, $u_{z}=-\psi _{x}$ the incompressibility
is identically satisfied and

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=G'(z)\sin kx\qquad 
u_{z}=-kG(z)\cos kx  ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[G''+k^{2}G\bigr]\sin kx\;, \qquad 
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[G'''-k^{2}G'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p-2\mu kG'\cos kx
        =\mu k\Bigl[\,k^{-1}G'''-3G'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $\nabla^{2}p=0$ the $x$– and $z$–momentum equations give
$(d^{2}/dz^{2}-k^{2})^{2}G=0$.  Putting $\zeta=kz$, $D=k h$

\begin{equation*}
g(\zeta)=G(z)=A(\zeta)\cosh\zeta+B(\zeta)\sinh\zeta,\qquad 
A=p_{0}+p_{1}\zeta,\;\;B=q_{0}+q_{1}\zeta ,
\end{equation*}
is the normal form.  The kinematic conditions at the rigid top

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;g'(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{z}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;g(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
give $p_{0}=0$ and $p_{1}+q_{0}=0$.  The condition
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
g'''(h)-3g'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow g(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}D\cosh D+\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}D\bigr)\sinh D=0 .
\end{equation*}
The prescribed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[G''(h)+k^{2}G(h)\bigr]=S_{0}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
k^{2}g''(D)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
whence with $q_{0}=-p_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=p_{1}\frac{\sinh D-D\cosh D}{D\sinh D}, 
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
g''(D)=p_{1}\frac{2\bigl[\sinh D\cosh D-D\bigr]}{D\sinh D}
       =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu k^{2}}
      \frac{D\sinh D}{2\bigl[\sinh D\cosh D-D\bigr]}\; .
\end{equation*}
At the free top $u_{x}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\mu k^{2}g'''(0)\cos kx
             =\mu k^{2}\bigl[\,3p_{1}+q_{0}\bigr]\cos kx
             =2\mu k^{2}p_{1}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx$ the steady deflection
is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu k^{2}p_{1}}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{D\sinh D}{\sinh D\cosh D-D}\;,
\qquad D=k h ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional isostatic restoring force $\Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= {\cal C}(k h)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
{\cal C}(D)=\frac{D\sinh D}{\sinh D\cosh D-D}
      =\frac{\,k h\sinh k h\,}{\sinh k h\cosh k h-kh}
      =\frac{2k h\sinh k h}{\sinh 2k h-2k h}
      =\frac{1}{\cosh k h-(k h)\csch k h}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/(\Delta\rho g)$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(D)=\frac{k h\sinh k h}{\sinh k h\cosh k h-kh}
      =\frac{1}{\cosh k h-kh\csch k h}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the (dimensionless) dynamic-topography transfer function.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(D)=\frac{D\sinh D}{\sinh D\cosh D-D}
       =\frac{2D\sinh D}{\sinh 2D-2D}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the long-wave, $k h\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
{\cal C}(D)=\frac{3}{2D}+O(D^{0}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{3}{2k h}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\approx 3S_{0}/2\Delta\rho gk h$ for $k h\ll1$, the
usual $1/kh$ growth of viscous dynamic topography.  With the
plate density $\rho$ used in the statement $\rho$ may be replaced
by $\Delta\rho$ and $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(D)=D\sinh D/[ \sinh D\cosh D-D]$.  This is the
conventional $ \chi(kh)=kh/[ \cosh kh-kh\csch kh]$ transfer
function, often written as
$e_{s}= S_{0}/(\Delta\rho g)\;[2kh\sinh kh/(\sinh2kh-2kh)]$.
Up to the trivial $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor this is the required
stable amplitude.  The $k h\ll1$ long-wave limit is the commonly quoted
$\chi\simeq3/2kh$, and with the usual $4/2$ lubrication normalisation
$\chi\simeq4/kh$. ∎

public_text.11:
For the linear Stokes problem write the stream function

$\psi(x,z,t)=\Phi (z,t)\sin kx\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
u_{x}=\Phi_{z}\sin kx\,,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx$ ,

so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is

$${\cal L}\Phi\equiv \Phi^{(iv)}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad{\cal L}=\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}  .
$$

Put

$\Phi(z)=(\alpha +\beta z)\cosh kz+(\gamma+\delta z)\sinh kz $ .

$u_{x}=0$ at $z=0$ gives $\Phi'(0)=0$, i.e. $\beta+k\gamma=0$, and

$u_{z}(0,t)=\dot e\cos kx$ gives $\Phi(0,t)=- \dot e/k$, i.e.
$\alpha=-\dot e/k$.  With the impermeable base $u_{z}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]\sin kx
=S_{0}\sin kx  ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the base $\Phi(h)=0$, $\Phi''(h)=S_{0}/\mu$.  With
$P(z)$ defined by $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''(z)-k^{2}\Phi'(z)\Big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(z)=-\Big[P(z)+2\mu k\Phi'(z)\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
     =\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e(t) .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi'(0)=0$ the expansion of $\Phi$ about $z=0$ gives
$\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}\gamma$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\gamma=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k^{2}}\,e(t),\qquad 
\beta=-k\gamma=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e(t) ,
\end{equation*}
and $\alpha=-\dot e/k$.  Using $\Phi(h)=0$ and $\Phi''(h)=S_{0}/\mu$
gives after elimination of $\delta$

\begin{equation*}
\gamma\big[\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\big]
+c^{2}\alpha+\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu}\,h\sinh kh=0 ,
\qquad c\equiv\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{2}\dot e+\frac{\rho g}{k}\big[\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\big]e
   =\frac{S_{0}h\sinh kh}{\mu}\,,
\end{equation*}
the quasi static evolution equation for the surface deflection
$e(t)\cos kx$.  The viscous relaxation time is
$\tau_{v}=2\mu c^{2}/[\rho g(k\,\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh)]$.
Putting $\dot e=0$ the steady topography is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{k\,h\sinh kh}
            {\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}\;
        \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{k h}{\cosh kh-\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\sinh kh}}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual isostatic notation $ \rho\to\Delta\rho$
the steady deflection can be written in the form
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the dimensionless
amplitude

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}
               {\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh}
        =\frac{kh}{\cosh kh-kh\,{\rm cosech}kh}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}
                -{\rm cosech}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
$1/\chi=( \cosh kh/kh)-{\rm cosech}kh$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq\frac{3}{2kh}\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad
\chi\simeq2kh\,e^{-kh}\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long-wavelength $3/2kh$ law.  With the notation used
in the statement the factor which multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is $\chi_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi$ and for the conventional
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the
$\chi$ quoted above, up to the inessential sign of the
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ phase.  The minus sign is often
written as $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ denoting a depression
under the positive basal shear; the magnitude is the $\chi$ given
above. ∎

public_text.15:
In the $x\!-\!z$ plane put $z=0$ at the deflected free surface and $z=h$
at the base,

\begin{equation*}
u=\Bigl(U(z)\sin kx ,\;W(z)\cos kx\Bigr) ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and, with $\psi(z)\sin kx$ the Stokes stream function,
\begin{equation*}
